.TH CLADENC 1 "" cladenc "cladenc"
.SH NAME
cladenc \- carries out a directed evolution of an agentic coding harness, in which harnesses are cloned, each diversifying its clone, and are evaluated
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
\fBcladenc\fP [\fB\-\-help\fP] [\fB\-\-version\fP]
\fBcladenc report\fP \fB\-\-outputs\-dir\fP <DIR> [\fB\-\-json\fP]
\fBcladenc resume\fP \fB\-\-outputs\-dir\fP <DIR>
\fBcladenc start\fP \fB\-\-outputs\-dir\fP <DIR> ...
\fBcladenc status\fP \fB\-\-outputs\-dir\fP <DIR> [\fB\-\-json\fP]
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
\fBcladenc\fP works by directed evolution.
A directed evolution starts from one harness, the primogenitor.
A new harness is made in two steps.
In a cloning, \fBcladenc\fP copies the source code of a harness, the
progenitor, and the copy is the progenitor's clone.
In a diversification, the progenitor changes its clone, and the clone that it
changed is its child.
Every harness is evaluated on every task of the datasets a person brings.
Dataset is Harbor's word for a collection of tasks.
A part of the tasks of each dataset is held out: no harness that diversifies a
clone reads about them.
The holdout tasks are divided in two, the selection tasks and the
corroboration tasks.
Artificial selection chooses which harness is cloned, and at the end names
the best harness, both by the selection tasks alone.
Nothing is chosen by the corroboration tasks, so they say how far the best
harness is ahead of the primogenitor on tasks that had no part in the
choosing.
.PP
\fBcladenc\fP does its work through a sub-command.
Called with neither a sub-command nor a flag, it prints what \fB\-\-help\fP
prints, on standard error, and exits with status 2.
\fB\-\-help\fP after a sub-command prints its flags, and the manual page of
the sub-command says the rest.
.SH SUB\-COMMANDS
.TP
\fBcladenc report\fP
Prints the report of a directed evolution that has ended, without changing it.
Its manual page is \fBcladenc\-report\fP(1).
.TP
\fBcladenc resume\fP
Continues a directed evolution that stopped before its end.
Its manual page is \fBcladenc\-resume\fP(1).
.TP
\fBcladenc start\fP
Starts a new directed evolution and prints the directory of its best harness.
Its manual page is \fBcladenc\-start\fP(1).
.TP
\fBcladenc status\fP
Prints the state so far of a directed evolution that has not ended, without
changing it.
Its manual page is \fBcladenc\-status\fP(1).
.SH FLAGS
.TP
\fB\-h\fP, \fB\-\-help\fP
Print help.
.TP
\fB\-V\fP, \fB\-\-version\fP
Print version.
.SH FILES
.TP
\fB/opt/cladenc/bin/cladenc\fP
The program, where the package of a release installs it.
A person calls the program by that full path, or adds /opt/cladenc/bin to PATH
and calls it as \fBcladenc\fP.
.TP
\fB/opt/cladenc/share/man\fP
The manual pages, where the package of a release installs them.
\fBman\fP finds them where /opt/cladenc/bin is on PATH, and is told where they
are otherwise, as \fBman \-M /opt/cladenc/share/man cladenc\-start\fP.
.SH SEE ALSO
\fBcladenc\-report\fP(1),
\fBcladenc\-resume\fP(1),
\fBcladenc\-start\fP(1),
\fBcladenc\-status\fP(1)
